auto-inc-dec: turn chains of reg+disp accesses into post-increments
[PR50749]

auto-inc-dec pairs one memory access with one increment.  Loops that
read several consecutive values per iteration reach it as

    *(r + 0); *(r + 4); *(r + 8); r = r + 12;

and none of the accesses pairs with the add, so SH2 kept displacement
addressing plus the add (and byte/word displacement loads tie up r0):

    before:  mov.l @r4,r1 / mov.l @(4,r4),r2 / mov.l @(8,r4),r0 / add #12,r4
    after:   mov.l @r4+,r1 / mov.l @r4+,r2 / mov.l @r4+,r0

New merge_inc_chain runs before the existing matching: for "r = r + C"
it walks back over the uses of r; if the last ones are MEMs at offsets
0, s1, s1+s2, ... (s = access size) adding up to exactly C, it rewrites
them all as (post_inc r) in one validated change group and deletes the
add.  Anything else using r in that range (a call, asm, a non-MEM use,
a second use in one insn, a REG_EQUAL note) stops it.  On SH a chain
containing a store fails validation (no post-increment stores) and is
left alone.  Debug insns in the range are rebased.

Not covered: pre-decrement store chains (pr50749-qihisi-predec-*.c) and
an increment into another register ("q = p + 12", pr50749-qihisi-
postinc-2.c).

Tested on sh-elf -m2 (GCC 14.2.0; auto-inc-dec.cc is identical in
14.3.0): gcc.c-torture/execute on the GDB SH simulator at -O1, -O2, -O3,
-Os, -O2 -funroll-loops, -Os -funroll-all-loops and -O3 -g: no result
changed (1374 passes per option set before and after).  gcc.c-torture
compile+execute at -O2 -g -fcompare-debug: no ICEs, no compare-debug
failures.  .text: -O2 -0.001%, -Os -0.002%, -O2 -funroll-loops -0.016%,
no file grew.  pr50749-qihisi-postinc-4.c now passes; its xfails are
removed.

Backported to 10.4.0 (applies with line offsets), tested together with
the other SH patches on sh-elf -m2: gcc.c-torture/execute at
-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os on the GDB SH simulator, no result changed
(1477/1475/1475/1473 passes); gcc.c-torture compile+execute: no ICEs;
-O2 -g -fcompare-debug: no new failures. pr50749-qihisi-postinc-4.c gets
its 3/3/3 post-increment loads.

